<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hinton St Mary Mosaic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hinton St Mary Mosaic is a fourth-century Roman floor mosaic found in 1963 at Hinton St Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its central roundel shows the head of a man in front of the chi-rho monogram, widely thought to be one of the earliest known images of Christ. It is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hinton-st-mary-mosaic.jpg" alt="Hinton St Mary Mosaic" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fransars · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic2_-_plw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity spread from the 3rd century, and the <b>Hinton St Mary</b> mosaic shows an early image of Christ.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
